In the discussion, James provided detailed descriptions of Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs): SSP1 (Sustainability), SSP2 (Middle of the Road), SSP3 (Regional Rivalry), and SSP5 (Fossil-Fueled Development). Each SSP outlines different global development trajectories and their environmental implications. James requested abbreviated descriptions for each SSP-RCP combo linked to the associated CO2 emissions and projected warming levels. I summarized each combo, highlighting the emissions outcomes and scenarios ranging from very low emissions (SSP1-1.9) resulting in 1.4°C warming to very high emissions (SSP5-8.5) resulting in 4.4°C warming by 2100, underscoring the critical relationship between societal choices and climate impacts.
